Original commit body from 7a3158f1ba8fcf952c970c674bac752466ac1bf8:

io_boundary._CATALOG_PARENTS declares four inheriting languages -- cpp <- c,
kotlin <- java, scala <- java, elixir <- erlang -- and io_boundary.load_catalog
honours it by merging the parent. The taint catalogue is derived from the SAME
YAML by _derive_auto_imports_from_io_primitives, which called
IoBoundaryCatalog.from_yaml(path) directly and inherited NOTHING, while its own
docstring claimed ambiguous_by_lang lets the taint matchers "disambiguate
exactly as io-boundaries does" (L50: a docstring asserting a parity that does
not hold).

MEASURED, catalogue-level. Taint sources+sinks:

    cpp        3  ->   70   (+67)   C++ ran taint on THREE primitives; C has 67
    elixir   231  ->  469  (+238)
    kotlin    26  ->  138  (+112)
    scala     23  ->  137  (+114)
    TOTAL   1588  -> 2119  (+531, a 33% increase in the whole catalogue)

ambiguous_names, the short-name collision guard, was missing for the same four:
cpp 0 -> 19, kotlin 34 -> 58, scala 73 -> 80, elixir 50 -> 54. So the inheriting
languages had BOTH a fraction of their primitive surface AND a weaker guard
against false matches on what remained.

THE PUBLISHED COVERAGE PERCENTAGE GOES DOWN, AND THAT IS THE FIX WORKING.
verify-claims' dataflow_coverage block now reports 740 sources / 1406 sinks (was
553 / 1062) with the data-flow-capable count unchanged at 274 -- none of the four
has a CFG mapping -- so coverage reads 19.5% where it read 25.8%. The earlier
figure was not bad arithmetic; it was computed over a catalogue missing 344
sinks it should have been matching. A coverage table that moves against you when
capability is added is doing the job it was added for.

ALSO FIXED: `- on` in ambiguous_names is a YAML 1.1 BOOLEAN. javascript.yaml and
scala.yaml each declared a bare `on`, which parses as True, so the string "on"
-- EventEmitter.on, the most common callback name in Node -- was never
suppressed in either language while the list looked correct. Both quoted, reason
recorded in place. A text grep cannot see this (the file reads `on` either way),
so the guard asserts against the LOADED value and scans every catalogue file
rather than the two known ones.

HONEST SCOPE. The 9-repo cohort contains no cpp, kotlin, scala or elixir source,
so no arm of it can observe any of this (L59 -- before running an A/B, check a
path exists by which the difference reaches the number). Evidence here is
catalogue-level and structural, asserted directly rather than inferred from a
cohort blind to it. What this predicts for real repos in those languages is
untested and is not claimed.

Verified: 12 tests red-first; core package 10924 passed; all 6 packages at 100%
in isolation; mypy ratchet clean; ruff clean.

